The finite-time-blowup test problem (doc/dfmm_3d.md Sec. 7, Stage 5), from
Downloads/before_blowup_ideal_gas_pedagogical.pdf.

Compile:
  cd bin && make COMPILER=METAL NDIM=3 HYDRO=1 DFMM=2 INIT=13 ramses   (moments)
  cd bin && make COMPILER=METAL NDIM=3 HYDRO=1        INIT=13 ramses   (Euler)
Both builds read this namelist; the DFMM_PARAMS block is simply ignored when
the binary was compiled with DFMM=0.  Do not write an ampersand-prefixed group
name anywhere in this header: gfortran's namelist reader will treat it as the
start of that group and then fail on the surrounding prose.

Axisymmetric straining flow, exactly divergence-free and periodic, with the
strain of the note's Eq. (19) at the box centre:
    S = (1/Delta) diag(-2,-2,4),  ||S||_op = 4/Delta,  t_def = Delta/4.
The strain tensor is diagonal EVERYWHERE in this field -- no shear strain
anywhere, though the vorticity is nonzero.  See hydro/condinit.f90,
#if INIT==BLOWUP.

VISCOSITY.  The note fixes no numerical value for mu; it takes the velocity
construction as given from its Ref. [1] and audits it.  What it does fix, and
what is reproduced here exactly, is the dilute hard-sphere model of its
Eqs. (15)-(16):
    mu = p_g * (5/4) t_c,   kappa = (15/4)(k_B/m) mu,   Pr = 2/3,
    nu = (5 pi/32) cbar lambda = 0.6068 c_s lambda.
In this scheme mu = p * dfmm_tau, so dfmm_tau = (5/4) t_c and
dfmm_prandtl = 2/3 IS the note's transport model.  There is no separate
viscosity number to import.

THE GOVERNING GROUP.  In code units boxlen = rho0 = p0 = 1, gamma = 5/3
(c_s = 1.29099), everything depends on one number:
    K = 8 mu / (p Delta) = 8 dfmm_tau / Delta = Delta_P / Delta
with Delta_P = 8 mu/p_g = 10 t_c the note's Eq. (25) threshold.  Then
    lam_min(P_NS)/p = 1 - K           (note Eq. 23; NS predicts negative
                                       absolute pressure for K > 1)
    t_c / t_def     = 0.4 K
    Kn              = 0.4944824 K / Ma        <-- forced by a one-mode field
    t_diss / t_def  = gamma Ma^2 / (2 K)

Those last two identities are why a fixed-flow ladder does not work.  A single
wavenumber ties Kn, K and Ma together: you cannot make K large while keeping
both Kn and Ma small.  Worse, at Ma = 0.3 the third identity gives
t_diss/t_def = 0.075 at K = 1, so the flow viscously dissipates thirteen times
faster than it deforms and Pi never equilibrates -- such a ladder returns a
spuriously benign null result with |Pi|/p falling as 1/K.

The ladder below therefore holds t_diss = t_def by setting Ma = 1.1 sqrt(K),
i.e. Delta = 0.4483/sqrt(K) and dfmm_tau = 0.056037 sqrt(K).  The price is
that the high-K rungs are transonic; that coupling is a property of the
one-mode initial condition, not of the scheme, and raising blowup_nwave trades
it against resolution.

   K     Ma     blowup_delta  dfmm_tau     Kn    lam_min(P_NS)/p   ten-moment
                                                    (Navier-Stokes)  prediction
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  0.10  0.348     1.4176      0.01772    0.142       +0.90           +0.905
  0.25  0.550     0.8966      0.02802    0.225       +0.75           +0.778
  0.50  0.778     0.6340      0.03962    0.318       +0.50           +0.600
  1.00  1.100     0.4483      0.05604    0.450        0.00           +0.333
  1.50  1.347     0.3660      0.06863    0.551       -0.50           +0.143
  2.00  1.556     0.3170      0.07925    0.636       -1.00            0.000
  3.00  1.905     0.2588      0.09706    0.779       -2.00           -0.200

The last column is the analytic fixed point of the ten-moment strain box for
this field.  With S = sigma diag(-1/2,-1/2,1) and Pi = y p diag(-1/2,-1/2,1),
the Sec. 4 source gives dPi_zz/dt = -2 p sigma - y p sigma - Pi_zz/tau, whose
steady state is
    Pi_zz/p = -2K/(2+K),   min lam(P)/p = (2-K)/(2+K),
    |Pi - Pi_NS| / |Pi| = K/2.
So the falsifiable claim this problem tests is: the Newtonian stress is already
50% wrong at K = 1, the note's own zero-crossing, and the evolved pressure
tensor does not reach the cone edge until K = 2 -- twice as far -- because the
nonlinear term -[Pi G]^dev saturates the anisotropy that the Newtonian
extrapolation grows without bound.  Run each rung with dfmm_closure='ns' and
'evolve' and compare against those two columns.

MEMORY.  Size ngridtot to the level, not generously: a uniform level L needs
sum_{l<=L} 2^(3l-3) ~ 1.15 * 2^(3L-3) octs, i.e. about 600 (L=4), 4700 (L=5),
37500 (L=6), 300000 (L=7).  ngridtot drives the allocation directly and a
DFMM=2 build carries four times the fields of plain hydro, so an
over-allocation is expensive: ngridtot=2000000 at level 5 measured 2.6 GB of
resident memory where ngridtot=20000 measured 92 MB, with bit-identical
results.  The values below are for level 4-5; raise them for level 6+.

Run for one deformation time: tout = Delta/4.  With Delta = 0.4483 that is
tout = 0.1121; set it per rung.

EXACT OUTPUT TIME.  tout_exact=.true. clips the coarse timestep so the
snapshot lands ON tout rather than on the first step past it.  Without it the
overshoot is up to a full dt -- 0.9% of t in a measured level-5 run -- and for
a blowup study the deliverable IS the state at a predicted time t_star, where
the realizability and Knudsen indicators change on the timestep scale.  With
it the landing is exact to 2e-14 relative.  It costs at most one shortened
step per output and is off by default, so set it in every run whose answer is
a value AT a time rather than a trend.

&RUN_PARAMS
hydro=.true.
ncontrol=10
nrestart=0
nremap=0
nsubcycle=10*1
verbose=.false.
nstepmax=100000
nsuperoct=2
/

&AMR_PARAMS
levelmin=5
levelmax=5
ngridtot=20000
ncachemax=10000
nexpand=1
boxlen=1.0
/

&INIT_PARAMS
nregion=1
region_type(1)='square'
x_center=0.5
y_center=0.5
z_center=0.5
length_x=10.0
length_y=10.0
length_z=10.0
exp_region=10.0
d_region=1.0
u_region=0.0
v_region=0.0
w_region=0.0
p_region=1.0
/

&BLOWUP_PARAMS
blowup_delta=0.4483
blowup_rho0=1.0
blowup_p0=1.0
blowup_nwave=1
blowup_init_ns=.false.
/

&OUTPUT_PARAMS
foutput=1000000   ! >0 so the tout-driven snapshot fires (amr_step.f90:107)
tout=0.1121
tout_exact=.true.   ! land the snapshot ON tout, not one step past it
/

&HYDRO_PARAMS
gamma=1.6666666666666667
courant_factor=0.8
slope_type=1
riemann='hll'
/

&DFMM_PARAMS
dfmm_tau=0.05604
dfmm_prandtl=0.6666666666666667
dfmm_source=.true.
dfmm_diag=.true.
dfmm_fatal_realizability=.false.
/
